Phase 8: Hardening (Ultimate Security & Compliance)

Goal: Finalize the system’s security with hardware attestation and formal verification, and prepare for compliance audits. This phase ensures no loopholes remain: even the platform and critical algorithms are trustworthy and verifiable.

 Hardware Attestation for Platform Integrity: Integrate with a TPM or secure enclave to attest the runtime environment. Develop an AttestationService that collects low-level measurements (e.g., TPM PCR values) and verifies them against expected values (baseline of a secure boot, code hash, etc.). On mismatch, log a SecurityAlert event (e.g., "Platform Tampering Detected") and consider halting or restricting operation. The PolicyEngine can call verify_platform_integrity() at startup or periodically, and potentially factor this into policy decisions (e.g., a policy could deny all critical actions if the platform is compromised). By doing this, we harden Forever Law and Sentinel Law at a root level – ensuring the machine itself hasn’t been subverted.

Deploy: On Windows, you might use a virtual TPM or remote attestation; ensure development can simulate this if physical TPM is not available.

