 Formal Verification of Critical Invariants: Use Rust formal verification tools (like Prusti or Kani/Verus) to prove key properties of the core algorithms. Focus on:

Signature Verification function: prove that if it returns OK, the signature indeed matches the content (no false positives).

Consent Validation function: prove that given a valid consent envelope and well-formed scope, if it allows an action, then that action is truly within the consent scope (and if outside scope, it denies).

Ledger Chain Integrity function: prove that after verification, every event links correctly to its predecessor (ensuring the append-only chain is unbroken).
Add contracts (#[requires], #[ensures]) to the Rust code and run the verifier to check these conditions. Address any issues the verifier finds (this may uncover edge-case bugs). Achieving these proofs provides mathematical assurance of Forever Law (integrity) and Sentinel Law (consent) enforcement at the code level.

